  "query_preview": "from the eastern Pacific, into the northern Plains. An \nupper level trough was situated over southeast Canada and into \nthe northern Great Lakes region.\n\nFor tonight, surface high pressure moves east and a southerly\nreturn flow develops from west to east across the forecast area.\nWinds have already turned southeast over western ND, and will\ncontinue to increase through the remainder of the afternoon.\nSoutheast winds may be breezy at times across the area tonight\nas the return flow works its way east. The dry lower atmosphere\nand breezy conditions will inhibit any fog tonight. Eastern\nportions of central ND may see lows drop down into the mid to\nupper 30s, as winds won't pick up here until near sunrise.\n\nAfter midnight, there may be some mid level cloudiness work its\nway northward from central South Dakota, into south central and\neastern North Dakota. A sprinkle or isolated brief shower can't\nbe completely ruled out, but am not going to add any mention at\nthis time. Any precip would be negligible.\n\nA warm front lifts across the area on Monday bringing very warm\ntemperatures to southwest North Dakota. Daytime highs Monday are\ncurrently forecast to be in the mid 80s in far southwest ND. The\nnormal high for Dickinson in early October is in the lower to\nmiddle 60s. It won't be as warm elsewhere, but it will mild\npretty much everywhere on Monday. Breezy south to southeast\nwinds will continue over central and into eastern ND, while in\nthe west, winds will drop off, then shift to the west to\nnorthwest over far western ND Monday afternoon behind a cold\nfront. Winds will shift west to northwest across central ND \nMonday evening behind the front. See the fire weather \ndiscussion below for more specific details regarding fire \nweather concerns Monday.\n\nTemperatures trend cooler Tuesday through Thursday as a broad\nnorthwest upper level flow develops from British Columbia Canada\ninto the northern Great Lakes region. A strong upper level jet\nworks its way into northeastern ND Tuesday into Wednesday and\nremains situated northwest through southeast across the area\nthrough Thursday. A few jet induced sprinkles or brief showers \ncan not be ruled out again, but with surface high pressure at\nthe surface, will not add any mention of rain. \n\nThe upper level jet lifts back north Friday into Saturday and we\nwarm up again ahead of a developing upper level trough over \nwestern Canada into the Pacific Northwest. This upper level\ntrough may bring much cooler temperatures and at least a chance\nof precipitation late in the weekend and into early next week.\nCurrently the NBM is depicting low to medium (20 to 40%)\nprecipitation chances Saturday night through Sunday night.\n\nCurrent cluster analysis is favoring a cooldown with at least\nsome low precipitation chances later in the weekend and into\nearly next week. Cluster analysis is also in decent alignment\nsuggesting that it's not \"if\" we have a cooldown, it's more of a\n\"to what extent\" will we cool down, and how long will the \ncooldown last before we warm back up. NBM ensemble temperature \nguidance for October 11th-13th shows quite a drop off in \ntemperatures at all observation sites, but also a very large \nspread in both low and especially high temperatures for this \ntimeframe. As for precipitation type, deterministic and ensemble\nmodels are both hinting at temperatures cold enough to support \nsome snow. We will have to see how things play out through the \nupcoming week.",
